sections.body:
Every Node project intended to be run, shared, or published has a package.json file at its root. It's the manifest that tells npm and other developers what the project is and what it needs. Running npm init in an empty directory generates a starting one interactively; in practice most developers write or copy one. The fields that matter for almost every project:

```json
{
  "name": "order-service",
  "version": "1.4.0",
  "main": "index.js",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "node index.js",
    "dev": "node --watch index.js",
    "test": "node --test"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "express": "^4.18.2"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "eslint": "^8.50.0"
  }
}
```

name and version identify the package (required if you ever plan to publish it, but conventional even for private apps that never will be). main points at the entry-point file that other code gets when it requires or imports this package by name (less relevant for an app you just run directly, essential for a package meant to be depended on by others). scripts defines named shell commands runnable via npm run <name> (start and test get a shortcut: npm start and npm test, no run needed). This is where most projects put their build, lint, test and dev-server commands behind memorable names, so contributors don't have to remember the full underlying command. dependencies and devDependencies are both installed locally by default, but they are treated very differently in production, covered further down. You rarely edit those two sections by hand: npm install express adds express to dependencies (with a caret range of whatever version is current), and npm install --save-dev eslint adds eslint to devDependencies.

The obvious way to build a file path is to concatenate strings with a slash: __dirname + '/data/' + filename. That works on macOS and Linux and breaks in subtle ways on Windows, where the native separator is a backslash. Even on POSIX systems, naive concatenation can produce doubled slashes or unresolved '..' segments that some tools handle inconsistently. The path module exists so you never have to think about the current OS's separator.

path.join(...segments) concatenates path segments using the correct separator for the current platform and normalizes the result (collapsing redundant slashes, resolving '.'/'..' segments), but it does not make the result absolute if the inputs weren't already. path.resolve(...segments) guarantees an absolute path: it processes segments from right to left, treating each as relative to the previous, until it hits one that's already absolute (or runs out, in which case it falls back to the current working directory). It simulates 'if you cd'd through these segments one by one from here, where would you end up.'

The most common use of path.join is combining it with __dirname (in CommonJS) or the reconstructed equivalent (in ESM) to build a path relative to the *current source file's location*, rather than to wherever the process happens to be running from:

```javascript
const path = require('path');
const fs = require('fs/promises');

async function load() {
  // WRONG: './data/report.json' resolves against process.cwd(),
  // which is wherever the process was LAUNCHED from, not where this file lives
  const brittle = await fs.readFile('./data/report.json', 'utf8');

  // RIGHT: always resolves relative to this source file, regardless
  // of what directory someone ran `node ...` from
  const reliable = await fs.readFile(
    path.join(__dirname, 'data', 'report.json'),
    'utf8'
  );
  return { brittle, reliable };
}
```

The first version works on your machine, then fails with an ENOENT ('no such file or directory') error when a teammate runs the same script from a different directory. This is a very common bug. A relative path string carries no information about the source file it's written in: it's always resolved against process.cwd(), the directory the process was started from, which has nothing to do with your file structure unless you always launch node from the same place.

The http core module implements HTTP in Node. Express and every other Node web framework is a layer of convenience on top of it. http.createServer((req, res) => { ... }) takes a handler function that's called once per incoming request, with two objects: req (the incoming request, a readable stream, carrying .url, .method, .headers) and res (the outgoing response, a writable stream that you control).

People who came from a framework first are often surprised that **you must call res.end()** to complete the response. There's no automatic 'the handler returned, so send whatever we have.' Forget it, and the client's connection hangs until it times out. Node can't know you're done with a response unless you say so, because res is a stream, and streams don't close themselves.

Reading a request body shows the stream nature even more sharply. In a framework, req.body is just there. With the raw http module, req is a stream of Buffer chunks that arrive over time as the client sends them. There is no synchronous 'get me the whole body' call, because the body may not have fully arrived by the time your handler starts. You collect it by listening for 'data' events (each delivering another chunk) and acting only once the 'end' event fires, which signals that the client has finished sending:

```javascript
function readBody(req) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const chunks = [];
    req.on('data', (chunk) => chunks.push(chunk));
    req.on('end', () => resolve(Buffer.concat(chunks).toString('utf8')));
    req.on('error', reject);
  });
}
```

This is the boilerplate that body-parsing middleware like Express's express.json() automates. Building it once by hand shows that it's ordinary code, not magic, and it shows what the helper leaves out: this version accepts a body of any size, so a client that streams gigabytes will fill your memory. Real body parsers enforce a limit (express.json() defaults to 100kb) and reject anything larger.

The http module has no concept of 'routes'. Every request, whatever its URL or method, invokes the same handler you gave to createServer. Even a small API means branching on req.method and req.url yourself:

```javascript
const http = require('http');

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');

  if (req.method === 'GET' && req.url === '/health') {
    res.statusCode = 200;
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ status: 'ok' }));
  } else if (req.method === 'GET' && req.url === '/users') {
    res.statusCode = 200;
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ users: ['Amy', 'Ben'] }));
  } else {
    res.statusCode = 404;
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ error: 'Not found' }));
  }
});

server.listen(3000);
```

This works, but look at how much is hand-rolled for two trivial routes: matching method AND path in every branch, setting the Content-Type header on every response, and a fallback for anything unmatched (skip that, and an unrecognized URL gets no response and hangs the client). There's still nothing for parsing query strings, extracting path parameters like an :id segment, parsing a JSON body (the readBody helper above would have to be called and its result JSON.parsed in every route that needs it), or catching a thrown error in a route handler so one bad request doesn't take down the server. The work grows with the number of routes, and a framework like Express closes that gap, which is why the next concept picks up from here.

examples.code:
const fs = require('fs');
const fsPromises = require('fs/promises');

// Synchronous: fine here, since this runs once at startup, before serving any traffic
const configSync = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('./config.json', 'utf8'));

// Callback-based: non-blocking, error-first convention
fs.readFile('./config.json', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  const configCb = JSON.parse(data);
  console.log(configCb);
});

// Promise-based: non-blocking, pairs with async/await
async function loadConfig() {
  const data = await fsPromises.readFile('./config.json', 'utf8');
  return JSON.parse(data);
}

const path = require('path');
const fs = require('fs/promises');

// Breaks as soon as `node app.js` is run from a different working directory
async function brittleLoad() {
  return fs.readFile('./data/report.json', 'utf8');
}

// Always resolves relative to THIS source file's location, regardless of cwd
async function reliableLoad() {
  const fullPath = path.join(__dirname, 'data', 'report.json');
  return fs.readFile(fullPath, 'utf8');
}

const http = require('http');

function readBody(req) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const chunks = [];
    req.on('data', (chunk) => chunks.push(chunk));
    req.on('end', () => resolve(Buffer.concat(chunks).toString('utf8')));
    req.on('error', reject);
  });
}

const server = http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');

  if (req.method === 'GET' && req.url === '/health') {
    res.statusCode = 200;
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ status: 'ok' }));
    return;
  }

  if (req.method === 'POST' && req.url === '/echo') {
    const rawBody = await readBody(req);
    const parsed = JSON.parse(rawBody);
    res.statusCode = 200;
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ youSent: parsed }));
    return;
  }

  res.statusCode = 404;
  res.end(JSON.stringify({ error: 'Not found' }));
});

server.listen(3000);

