sections.body:
Every middleware function receives a third argument, conventionally named next, which is itself a function. Calling next() hands control to the next matching middleware in the stack. Express doesn't move on just because your function returned; it moves on because you called next(). That one mechanism drives the whole pipeline:

```javascript
function logger(req, res, next) {
  console.log(`${req.method} ${req.url}`);
  next();   // hand off to whatever comes next in the stack
}
```

Getting this wrong produces two common bugs. **Not calling next() (and not sending a response)** leaves the request stuck: Express is waiting for the middleware to decide what happens next, nothing tells it, and the client's connection hangs until it times out, like the missing res.end() bug from the raw http module one layer up. **Calling next() after already sending a response** (or calling it twice) usually fails later with an error such as 'Cannot set headers after they are sent' when a subsequent handler tries to respond, because each step should make one decision: finish the response yourself (res.send(), res.json(), res.end()) or hand off with next(), not both and not neither. One special case is worth knowing early: calling next(someError), passing an argument, tells Express this middleware failed, and instead of going to the next *normal* middleware, Express skips ahead to the nearest error-handling middleware (covered in the last section).

Middleware run in the order they were registered with app.use/app.get/etc, not by some inferred priority, not alphabetically, and not 'security-related things first.' An authentication check meant to protect a route must be registered *before* that route's handler in the source code, not after:

```javascript
// BROKEN: the route handler runs and responds BEFORE the auth check ever gets a turn
app.get('/admin/dashboard', dashboardHandler);
app.use(requireAuth);

// FIXED: auth check runs first; only calls next() (letting the request continue)
// if the request is actually authenticated
app.use(requireAuth);
app.get('/admin/dashboard', dashboardHandler);
```

In the broken version, an unauthenticated request to /admin/dashboard matches dashboardHandler first because it was registered earlier, and since the handler responds without calling next(), requireAuth never runs for that request. The same rule explains why body-parsing middleware like express.json() has to be registered before any route handler that reads req.body: if the handler runs first, req.body hasn't been populated yet.

Express identifies **error-handling middleware** by its function *arity*: four parameters, (err, req, res, next), instead of the usual three. That affects ordering: an error middleware only catches errors from middleware registered *before* it, since Express walks the stack in registration order and only consults later entries once something earlier calls next(err). That's why error-handling middleware is conventionally registered last, after every normal route and middleware; registered earlier, it would never see errors from routes defined after it.

examples.code:
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use(express.json());

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log(`${new Date().toISOString()} ${req.method} ${req.url}`);
  next();
});

app.get('/health', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ status: 'ok' });
});

app.post('/users', (req, res) => {
  const { name } = req.body;
  res.status(201).json({ id: 1, name });
});

app.listen(3000);

function requireAuth(req, res, next) {
  if (!req.headers.authorization) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Unauthorized' });
  }
  next();
}

// BROKEN: unauthenticated requests reach the handler and get the data back
app.get('/admin/dashboard', (req, res) => res.json({ secrets: 'yes' }));
app.use(requireAuth);

// FIXED: requireAuth runs first and can short-circuit before the handler runs
app.use(requireAuth);
app.get('/admin/dashboard', (req, res) => res.json({ secrets: 'yes' }));

app.get('/risky', (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const result = 10 / getUntrustedDivisor();   // may throw
    res.json({ result });
  } catch (err) {
    next(err);   // hands off to error-handling middleware instead of crashing
  }
});

// Registered LAST, after every other route and middleware
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error('Unhandled error:', err.message);
  res.status(500).json({ error: 'Something went wrong' });
});

